  "answer_text": "Great question! There are actually several key reasons why condiment packets don't need refrigeration, even though their larger jar or bottle counterparts often do. Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd12 Airtight Sealing\nThe tiny packets don't need to be refrigerated because they are sealed airtight. This seal is critical \u2014 it prevents bacteria, oxygen, and moisture from getting in, all of which are the main drivers of spoilage. Condiments like mayo are shelf-stable after being packaged in sterile, airtight containers. That being said, the sterile environment disappears once you open the jar (or packet).\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Preservatives\nBecause of the preservatives used in many condiments, which are intended to keep them safe for eating, most everyday condiments are generally good for several months to a year if packaged and stored correctly. Fast food condiment packets like mayo, ketchup, and mustard are typically shelf-stable until opened due to preservatives. Unopened packets do not require refrigeration and can be stored at room temperature for several months.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Acidity as a Natural Preservative\nMany of these condiments contain naturally acidic ingredients that inhibit bacterial growth. The acidic ingredients in ketchup prevent it from going bad if kept at room temperature after opening. Similarly, although the acid in mayonnaise makes it more difficult for bacteria to grow, this condiment can still go bad when left outside of the fridge once the seal is broken.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83e\udd0f Single-Use Portion Size\nThey're such a small portion that most people use them all up in one go. This means there's virtually no risk of repeated exposure to air and contaminants that would cause spoilage over time \u2014 which is the main concern with a large, repeatedly opened jar.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \u23f3 They Still Have a Shelf Life\nIt's worth noting that packets don't last *forever*. Ketchup packets, like the ones you find at McDonald's, Wendy's, Burger King, and other fast food spots, are generally good for about a year. Sauces that contain egg yolk are only good for about a year since fatty ingredients like eggs and butter spoil more quickly. And of course, once opened, mayonnaise packets should be refrigerated promptly and used within a day or two to prevent bacterial growth.\n\n---\n\n**In summary:** It's the combination of **airtight sterile packaging**, **preservatives**, **natural acidity**, and **tiny single-use portions** that allows condiment packets to be safely stored at room temperature \u2014 not magic! Once that seal is broken, the same rules apply as with any opened condiment.",
